Here's the formal test smell audit of your integration test suite using the **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog**.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 2              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **5**          |

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` | **Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // 3-second hard-coded wait
```

**Why it's a smell even for integration tests:** Hard sleeps cause non-deterministic timing failures and bloat suite duration. The async event may complete in 50ms or may need 5s under load — a fixed sleep handles neither case well.

**Fix:**
```csharp
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
{
    await Task.Delay(50, cts.Token);
}
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures; inflated suite runtime (3s per invocation unconditionally).

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` | **Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Why:** One branch will never execute for a given data setup, creating a hidden testing gap. The test always passes but verifies different properties depending on runtime type — you can't tell which path ran from the result alone.

**Fix:** Write two explicit tests:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com")!;
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Silent coverage gap — one branch is dead code in the test.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` | **Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // No assertions
}
```

**Why:** MSTest reports this as "passed" as long as no exception is thrown. A bug that silently corrupts data or inserts zero rows would go undetected.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — 100% pass rate with zero behavioral verification.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` | **Methods:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38), `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` (lines 51–52)

```csharp
// InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase — 4 assertions, no messages
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Why:** When one of multiple message-less assertions fails, the MSTest output shows only `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>` with no context about *which logical property* was being verified.

**Fix:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip through persistence");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip through persistence");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Database should assign a positive ID");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower diagnosis when CI fails — developers must read source to identify which property was wrong.

---

## Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The following tests are well-designed:

| Method | Why it's good |
|--------|--------------|
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single concern, clear arrange-act-assert, appropriate assertion |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count assertion is contextually obvious (3 items added → expect 3), uses `CollectionAssert` |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Tests a real concurrency concern with proper `await`, verifiable assertion |

The overall fixture design (in-memory SQLite, `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]`) is appropriate — no General Fixture smell since both fields are used by every test.

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | Add count/content assertion to `BulkInsert` | ~2 min |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | Split into two type-specific tests | ~5 min |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with polling + timeout | ~10 min |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | Add message strings to multi-assert methods | ~5 min |

Start with #1 — it's the highest risk-to-effort ratio (a test that currently proves nothing).
